    public string StrHelpDustVisibility { get; set; } = "Display stretch of the dust: the softening of an asinh curve over the accumulated optical depth, relative to the brightest dust in view (it adapts to the view like an eye). From 0.00001 to 1, on a logarithmic scale.\n\nSmaller: the faint, old tail and trail (thousands of times fainter than the coma) become visible while the coma saturates.\n1: close to linear, physically proportional brightness.\n\nVisual only: the simulated dust is unchanged.";
    public string StrHelpDustFlow { get; set; } = "Brightness marks riding the dust along synchrones (the dust of one emission instant), a motion cue near the nucleus: peak 4×, trough 0.15×, mean 1.\n\nOff by default: seen from 0.03 AU and beyond, the marks are the synchrone fan itself, hard rays through the nucleus that the physical optical depth does not show.\n\nVisual only. Their speed is set by the flow time-lapse.";
    public string StrHelpDustRhoNorm { get; set; } = "Divides the dust brightness by the distance from the nucleus (ρ), so the bright inner coma and the faint outer jets share one exposure.\n\nDisplay only: the simulated dust is unchanged.";
    public string StrHelpDustTrajectories { get; set; } = "Draws as lines the paths of about 200 grains since they left the nucleus, one colour per jet.";
    public string StrHelpDustExposure { get; set; } = "Brightens (+) or darkens (−) the dust over the automatic exposure, in photographic stops (EV), from −6 to +6 in steps of 0.25. One stop doubles or halves the brightness.";
    public string StrHelpDustTracers { get; set; } = "Draws one dust cluster in 256 as bright dots at the exact positions of its grain sizes, from the slow large grains to the fast small ones: the dots span the tail.\n\nThe dust drifts at 2 to 150 m/s while a pixel of a wide view spans tens of km: at low simulation speed it moves far less than a pixel per second, and the dense dots blend into a still fog. Tracers are real particles to follow (raise the simulation speed to see them stream).\n\nVisual only.";
    public string StrHelpDustFlowSpeed { get; set; } = "Speed of the brightness marks (flow pulses) that ride the dust while the simulation runs; they freeze on pause. From 1 to 10000, on a logarithmic scale.\n\n1: the marks move exactly with the dust (its real speed on screen, often far below a pixel per second in wide views).\nK: every mark moves K times faster than its dust, in its direction, so the whole swarm keeps its true velocity field at a readable pace. Changing it never jumps the marks.\n\nVisual only.";
    public string StrHelpGizmo { get; set; } = "The wireframe sphere and axes around the nucleus. Saved with the file.\n\nThe boxes below choose its parts:\n• Grid: the latitude / longitude wireframe;\n• Body X/Y: the body-fixed X (red) and Y (green) axes, rotating with the nucleus;\n• Spin axis: the rotation axis (body Z, blue);\n• Sun: the arrow towards the Sun;\n• Velocity: the comet's heliocentric velocity direction (magenta).";
    public string StrHelpJetAngles { get; set; } = "Draws the selected jet's latitude and longitude in the viewport as arcs, with their values in degrees. Saved with the file.";
